thing's  okay you know and they were asking you  were asking for spike to do as you know  as his characters Mookie to to do maybe  add and then  dressed to the camera on a stoop in  Brooklyn do you remember that spike hell  no oh yeah they didn't want they don't  want the character to pick up the money  that that sounded thrown at him and I  remember the two of us are coming back  on the plane after showing it to the  studio and they said now he can't pick  up their money and I said now you've  been broken I've been broke I said you  might for a moment say oh you know I'm  not gonna hell with us out but before  you walk away you don't have a job  you're gonna pick up that money and then  and then finally spike came up with the  idea of using those quotes by by Martin  Luther King Luther King and Malcolm X  and and and funny enough is that the  character of smiley didn't exist in the  script and so you look at it and you  think of course he must have because he  was so integral to the whole story and  that and that it came he thread his way  all the way through and and that and  that card he was holding those pictures  turned out to be so important in  wrapping the movie up but that was  really for the end of the movie that was  really the debate you know we always  have the attitude to just make the best  use of what we had not to think about  what we didn't have or who we didn't  have there was a version of this movie  that had a different cast had a  different budget had a different studio  and that quickly went by the wayside  when all those people said no because we  were going to make the movie anyway and  you know bill brought up a really great  point about just becoming the character  and to me as much as the movie is  anchored in a time and place that's so  real it also transcends time and place  and it's very emotional for me to see  the movie and see everybody here but I  also think that this movie is going to  live another hundred and twenty-five  years that's how important it is  and it reflects not just this  neighborhood and that time and place but  what the human race is its flaws and the  beauty of it and I'm just so proud to be  part of this film part of this legacy  part of this history and to thank Spike  for the courage that it took to make it  because it was so on time and artists  are often called on to reflect a time  and place but it isn't very often that  they have the courage to do it with such  urgency and and honesty and thank you  spike and keep it going John
 

